WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:04.810
Disney's animators have returned to Arendelle
for their second short film featuring the

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.010
characters from their smash-hit Frozen!

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.980
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'll be revealing some cool secrets

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.280
about Olaf's Frozen Adventure as well as hidden
details and Disney easter eggs you may have

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.670
missed in the new animated mini-movie!

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.179
I'm also running a Disney Funko Pop giveaway
on this video.

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:24.970
To enter, make sure you're a subscriber and
leave a comment below about this short film

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.580
or any of the Frozen movies.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.960
When it came to depicting Christmas celebrations
in Olaf's Frozen Adventure, the filmmakers

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.350
looked to Scandinavian culture, especially
Norway, for inspiration, just like they did

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:37.440
with Frozen.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.751
And that's why you'll find tons of goats dotted
around throughout the film, for example, on

00:00:41.751 --> 00:00:45.880
wrapping paper, as festive ornaments, and
even on Anna's dress.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.670
You see, in Nordic countries, the Yule goat
is a Christmas tradition that goes back many,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.039
many centuries and, though its meaning has
changed over time, it's still popular there

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:57.879
today as a Christmas ornament.

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:01.460
As well as all those pictures of goats in
the movie, originally there was also going

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.030
to be a goat character called Mr Jingles,
who got his name because he swallowed a bell,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.640
which meant he made jingling noises all the
time.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.690
Mr Jingles would have been part of Olaf's
journey around Arendelle and was going to

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.750
eat all the items Olaf collected in his sleigh.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.000
However, in the end, the directors decided
to drop Mr Jingles from the final film as

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.159
he was taking too much focus off Sven and
Olaf.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.140
Hidden among the many gifts that Olaf gathers
on his travels is a sweet little easter egg

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.540
to Disney's short film The Ballad of Nessie,
which was made by the same people who directed

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.549
Olaf's Frozen Adventure!

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:38.710
Many thanks to all the eagle-eyed people who
pointed Nessie out to me on my video for the

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.579
film's trailer!

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:44.840
The scene where Olaf gulps down an entire
fruitcake is a shout-out to another short

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.040
film made by the directors of Olaf's Frozen
Adventure.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.540
In the Disney holiday specials Prep &amp; Landing,
which are about a high-tech team of elves,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.210
there are several jokes about fruitcake, so
the filmmakers decided to continue that tradition

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.189
in Olaf's new short film.

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:04.289
Another festive tradition that Olaf discovers
during his adventure is the feast of Saint

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:09.110
Lucia, which in a number of Nordic countries
is celebrated on 13th December, and includes

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.110
a girl typically wearing a white gown and
red sash together with a wreath of candles

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.110
on her head.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.440
As well as being a fun moment, the scene where
Olaf gets a candy cane actually includes a

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.640
couple of cool Disney easter eggs.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.180
When Olaf replaces his carrot nose with the
candy cane and cries out, "Sugar Rush!", it's

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.500
a call-back to the scene in the original Frozen
movie where Olaf got his first carrot nose

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.500
from Anna.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.300
"We make candy canes together."

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.300
"Oooh!

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.300
He he he!"

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.300
"Sugar rush!"

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.300
"Oh darn!"

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.300
"Wooh!"

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.270
"I'm sorry, I, I was just ..."
"Head rush!"

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:45.620
On top of that, the scene is also a clever
shout-out to Disney's Wreck-It Ralph film

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.650
where Ralph enters a go-kart-racing candy-filled
game called Sugar Rush which features a Candy

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.770
Cane Forest.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.670
Wreck-It-Ralph 2 is Disney's next animated
movie, so it's nice to see it get a nod here.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:01.341
There's also a touch of adult humour in this
scene as Olaf's spinning eyes remind me of

00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:05.310
the way characters in movies like Pinocchio
and The Good Dinosaur react when they get

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.920
rather different rushes.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.810
As the star of the short film, Olaf gets some
fun new looks with a variety of festive accessories

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:18.180
including a bowtie made from ice which mirrors
the design of Elsa's signature snowflake and

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.110
highlights the strong connection between Elsa
and her favourite little snowman.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:26.570
If you look carefully at Elsa and Anna's dresses
in this film, you'll notice lots of small

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.200
but important details that are full of meaning.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.560
For example, the animators deliberately chose
similar colours for the sisters' clothes to

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.680
emphasise just how close they've become and
to reflect the fact that the sisters team

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.030
up in this film in contrast to Frozen where
they had to spend so much time apart.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.190
Elsa's gown is bluey-grey and has a jewelled
bodice which includes a design based on her

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.530
snowflake.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.860
The animators spent a lot of time making sure
the snowflake and diamond-shaped gems on the

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.150
dress reflected light in a realistic way so
they'd look clear and sparkly.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:02.970
But after all their hard work, the gems looked
so sparkly that they were actually a bit distracting,

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.400
so they had to dial back the shine a bit!

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.580
Also, if you look at the fur that tops Elsa's
gown, scattered within it you'll see little

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:14.720
frosted holiday berries, which are a reminder
of Elsa's icy powers; and you can also see

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.209
them as accessories in both Elsa and Anna's
hair, again emphasising the closeness of the

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.720
Frozen sisters.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.270
As for Elsa's snowflake, that also features
on her cape in a pattern that foreshadows

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.340
the beautiful ice tree she makes in the film.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.560
As for Anna's clothes, the dusty blue-violet
colour of her new outfit complements the dusty

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.370
blue-grey colour of her sister's gown.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.630
And little aspects of Anna's costume also
point to the fact that she and Kristoff are

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.960
now together, for example, take a look at
the sashes they wear in the film.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.790
If you listen closely to the film's score,
for instance, when Elsa and Anna remember

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:53.370
their childhood, you'll hear the return of
some familiar themes from Arendelle, however,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:58.440
composer Christophe Beck has given those musical
themes a new twist, adding a warm and cosy

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.290
feel so that they fit the new holiday setting.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.590
As for the new songs by Kate Anderson and
Elyssa Samsel, you won't just hear them sung

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.510
in the film, you'll also hear their music
woven into the score.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.420
For example, the opening track kicks off with
a little bit of "When We're Together" and

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:18.180
that melody is used throughout the film, the
idea being that when you finally hear the

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.600
characters sing the song towards the end,
it already feels comforting and familiar,

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.690
like any good holiday song!

